 Senate map is more contested: the quantitative outlook puts Democratic control chances near 78 percent, while markets sit closer to 68 percent, reflecting a narrower band of competitive states. Governorships lean Democratic as well, with most races rated likely or safe for the party. Late-cycle turbulence, including a federal lawsuit seeking to block certification of the Alaska Senate race if the Republican incumbent loses, adds procedural risk to the post-election period. Heavy spending in states like Michigan and tight polling in Texas and elsewhere keep several outcomes genuinely open. The elevated uncertainty multiplier reflects both the litigation headlines and the gap between model and market views on the Senate.", "key_factors": ["Generic ballot averages a roughly 8.7-point Democratic margin", "Prediction markets price Democratic House control above 90 percent but Senate control near 68 percent, below quantitative estimates", "DOJ lawsuit over Alaska Senate certification introduces post-election legal risk", "Heavy spending in Michigan and competitive polling in Texas, Maine, and New Hampshire widen the Senate battlefield", "23 House tossup seats leave room for a polling-error swing in either direction"], "chambers": {"house": "Democrats are strong favorites for House control, with markets above 90 percent and a seat range centered well above the majority threshold.", "senate": "Democrats are favored for Senate control, but markets at 68 percent run below quantitative estimates near 78 percent, leaving meaningful two-party risk.", "governor": "Democrats are clear favorites to hold or expand their governorship count, with most competitive races rated at least lean Democratic."}, "environment_adjustment": 0.0, "uncertainty_multiplier": 1.2}}